hi this is wesley from google cloud and  welcome to serverless migration station  a serverless expedition mini-series  dedicated to helping developers  modernize their apps to using the latest  features available on one of our  serverless compute platforms  i'm joined by serverless expedition host  martin and our little friend porter the  train who will take us on our continuing  journey migrating from app engine blob  store to cloud storage  sounds great and glad to be here was  last time in module 15 we added app  engine blob store to our sample app what  are we doing today  now that we have blob store usage in our  app today's exercise is to migrate it to  cloud storage  check out the module 15 video and code  lab because we pick up from where it  leaves off in addition to cloud storage  we're doing three other migrations at  the same time  now wait wes did you really say we're  doing  one plus three that is four migrations  today why on earth would you do that  true it does sound like a lot the  primary focus is on app engine blobster  to cloud storage because we've already  covered the others for example moving  from the web app to framework to flask  is covered in module one and migrating  from app engine adb to cloud nab is  addressed by module two when moving to  the standalone cloud services we get the  bonus that they're available in python 3  meaning we can finally upgrade from  python 2. in fact the updated app will  run on both python 2 and 3 to help with  those migration efforts  okay that makes more sense we're trying  to help developers upgrade from app  engine's legacy bundle services to the  standalone cloud versions that's right  and this migration is no different  moving from bundled services like  blobstore and ndb for data store access  to standalone equivalents like cloud  storage or cloud ndb or cloud datastore  makes your apps much more portable in  case you ever need to upgrade to the  latest app engine runtimes or move your  code to our sister serverless platforms  like cloud functions or cloud run  sounds good i'm going to do this by hand  while you're explaining it to so point  me in the right direction for sure  martin links to everything are down  below earlier we recommended doing the  module 15 code lab so start with your  code from that exercise if you don't  have it clone the repo or download the  zip file and grab it from the module 15  folder follow the module 16 codelab as i  go through the migration and when  complete your code should basically  match what's in the module 16 folder  ready let's go to the computer and walk  through this migration  start by confirming you've got a working  module 15 app there are no third party  libraries we need to bundle so no pip  install nor loop folder here just run  gcloud app deploy to upload to the cloud  hit your app engine app see the form  upload a file or skip it and get to the  most recent visits page it works so now  we can update the app let's start with  the config files namely app.yaml in  module 15 we use the jinda2 built-in  library but because we're switching from  web app2 to flask and jinja comes with  flask delete this we're adding use of  cloud client libraries those require grp  cio and setup tools so add those cloud  storage in particular needs ssl so add  that too and select the latest versions  of all of these  if you're also porting to python 3  uncomment the line at the top and delete  all remaining lines in app.yaml because  there are no built-in libraries in the  second generation runtimes as they'll be  installed automatically one more time  for python 3. app.yaml should only have  the one line for the runtime and that's  it going back to python 2 we're now  using third-party libraries built-in and  non-built-in or those we need to vendor  bundle or copy with our app these  include flask and the cloud client  libraries put them in requirements.txt  the presence of both built-in and  vendored third-party libraries means we  need to add an app engine config.py file  with at least these lines in it this  file has been featured in many of the  other migrations covered in this series  if this is new to you see the installing  libraries for python 2 apps linked below  regardless create both files with the  contents you see here if reporting to  python 3 there's no rendering nor use of  app engine config.py and all third-party  libraries go into requirements.txt  that's it for config let's look at the  app itself at the top swap out all the  imports web app 2 is replaced by flask  instead of using gender 2 from web app  to extras it's built into flask app  engine blob store and andy b are  replaced by cloud adb and cloud storage  respectively the blobster handlers in  webup are replaced by a combo of the i o  standard lib module flask and worksug  utilities by default blobster writes to  a cloud storage bucket named after your  app's url you know project  id.appspot.com  because we're doing a direct port with  the cloud storage client library  google.auth is used to get the project  id to specify the exact same bucket name  as blobstore but realize you can change  it  the next block of code is to replace the  base handler specifying the use of jinja  2 as a template engine this is  unnecessary as jinja 2 is the default in  flask on the module 16 side we need to  instantiate the flask app as well as  create api clients for cloud ndb and  cloud storage finally we put together  the bucket name as described a moment  ago  creating a new visit and querying the  most recent visits doesn't really change  much  one difference is that data store calls  must be wrapped in a width block  affecting both store visit and fetch  visits  review the module 2 collab and video if  you need more info here  the other change is that because we're  no longer using blob store we can't use  a blob key property instead we'll just  exchange it for a file name string for  us here in module 16 things get really  interesting the rest of the way and  replacing use of the upload and download  handlers  the next step is to refactor the upload  handler instead of a class it's just a  function in flask the post method  becomes a decorator argument both check  if a file was uploaded or not if there  is a file upload it to cloud storage  then create a visit with all the  necessary info finally do a 307 redirect  to slash or home  for the view or download handler replace  the class with another flask function  both take the blob or file requested as  an argument the web app routes are at  the bottom while flask routes are all  decorators but you can see the calls to  slash view with the blob or file info as  part of the path it's then parsed by  each handler and requested from cloud  storage oh yeah and return a 404 if not  found  the lobster handlers do a bit more of  the dirty work hence why the  replacements have a little bit more code  the last thing to take care of is the  main handler both git and post methods  are merged into one function the get  provides an upload url while the post  fetches the most recent visits  regardless which are called the template  is rendered with the provided data since  both frameworks use jinja 2 no changes  are needed in the web template that  concludes the required updates to the  main application and the initial config  changes  since we're using third-party libraries  run the pip install command to copy the  third-party libraries into the lib  folder now upload to app engine with  gcloud app deploy and confirm the app  works identically to the module 15  version meaning an initial form with  optional file upload followed by the  most recent visits page  if it works you will have successfully  completed all four migrations by the way  the app is fully python 3 compatible now  make the earlier suggested change to  app.yaml to enable python3 delete both  app engine config.py and the lib folder  run gcloud app deploy and boom you've  got a live running 